<h1>POLICE CRIMINAL RECORD SYSTEM</h1><p class='subtitle'>User Guide: From Login to Daily Operations</p><p>SOFTINFRA TECH - Directorate of Information Technology and Technological Traces (DITT)<br/>Prepared from the implemented React and Node.js application | 11 September 2026</p>
<h2>Purpose</h2><p>This guide explains how an authorized user operates the Police Criminal Record System (PCRS). The application centralizes case files, persons of interest, criminal records, wanted alerts, reporting, and traceability across the national network.</p>
<h2>1. Before You Start</h2><ol><li>Start PostgreSQL and ensure the PCRS database is available.</li><li>Start the Node.js backend from <b>server</b> with <b>npm start</b>.</li><li>Start the React frontend from <b>client</b> with <b>npm run dev</b>.</li><li>Open <b>http://127.0.0.1:5173/</b> in Chrome.</li></ol><p>The application uses role-based access: Police Officer, Judicial Authority, General Inspectorate, and Administrator.</p>
<h2>2. Login and Authentication</h2>
<p class='figure'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' /><br/><i>Figure 1. PCRS login page.</i></p>
<p>The login page authenticates an active user before allowing access to sensitive data.</p><ol><li>Enter the email address.</li><li>Enter the password.</li><li>Select <b>Access console</b>.</li><li>The system creates a secure session and opens the operational console.</li></ol><p class='note'><b>Demo accounts</b><br/>Police Officer: officer@softinfra.cm / Officer123!<br/>Judicial Authority: judge@softinfra.cm / Judge123!<br/>Inspectorate: inspector@softinfra.cm / Inspector123!<br/>Administrator: admin@softinfra.cm / Admin123!</p>
<h2>3. National Overview Dashboard</h2>
<p class='figure'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' /><br/><i>Figure 2. National overview dashboard.</i></p>
<p>The dashboard provides the national situation report.</p><ol><li>Review total case files.</li><li>Review active investigations.</li><li>Review wanted persons.</li><li>Review pending authorizations.</li><li>Compare case statuses in the Case status distribution panel.</li><li>Review recent activity for traceability.</li></ol>
<h2>4. Case Files</h2>
<p>Select <b>Case files</b> in the left navigation. Search by title, reference number, or description. Select <b>Register case</b> to create an investigation.</p>
<p class='figure'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' /><br/><i>Figure 3. Register case form.</i></p>
<ol><li>Enter the case title and offence nature.</li><li>Select the incident date.</li><li>Enter region and location.</li><li>Describe the incident.</li><li>Select <b>Submit securely</b>. The server generates the reference number and records the action.</li></ol>
<h2>5. Persons of Interest</h2>
<p class='figure'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' /><br/><i>Figure 4. Person-of-interest register.</i></p>
<ol><li>Select <b>Persons of interest</b>.</li><li>Search by identification number, name, or alias.</li><li>Select <b>Register person</b> to record identity and descriptive information.</li><li>Review whether the person is marked Wanted or Clear.</li></ol><p>A person may be linked to several cases, criminal records, and alerts.</p>
<h2>6. Criminal Records</h2>
<p class='figure'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' /><br/><i>Figure 5. Criminal-history register.</i></p>
<ol><li>Select <b>Criminal records</b>.</li><li>Search by person or offence.</li><li>Select <b>Add record</b> for a new offence history.</li><li>Enter the person ID, offence, dates, location, sentence, court, and notes.</li><li>Verify or flag the record after review.</li></ol>
<h2>7. Wanted Alerts and Judicial Authorization</h2>
<p class='figure'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' /><br/><i>Figure 6. Wanted-alert register and lifecycle actions.</i></p>
<p>Alerts follow the lifecycle pending, authorized, issued, resolved, rejected, or cancelled.</p><ol><li>Select <b>Wanted alerts</b>.</li><li>Search for an existing alert.</li><li>Select <b>Request alert</b>.</li><li>Enter person ID, optional case ID, reason, priority, description, and last known location.</li><li>A Judicial Authority or Administrator selects <b>Authorize</b> or <b>Reject</b>.</li><li>An authorized alert can be <b>Issued</b>.</li><li>A Police Officer can select <b>Resolve</b> when the alert is no longer active.</li></ol><p>Every lifecycle action is protected by role-based authorization and recorded in the audit log.</p>
<h2>8. Administration and Auditability</h2><ol><li>Administrators open <b>Users</b> to review personnel and roles.</li><li>Administrators create, update, or deactivate accounts according to institutional policy.</li><li>Administrators open <b>Audit trail</b> to review who performed an action, which entity was affected, and when.</li></ol>
<h2>9. Signing Out and Troubleshooting</h2><ol><li>Select <b>Sign out</b> in the top-right corner.</li><li>If data is missing, confirm Node.js is running on port 5000 and PostgreSQL is running.</li><li>If Chrome cannot open the app, start Vite and use http://127.0.0.1:5173/.</li><li>For login problems, verify the demo credentials and test http://localhost:5000/api/health.</li></ol>
<h2>10. Security Notes</h2><p>Use only authorized accounts. Do not use real confidential criminal data in development. Change demo passwords before controlled deployment, keep JWT secrets private, and restrict database access to trusted personnel.</p>
</body></html>
